ructures.” Discrete Mathematics\n110 (1992), 43–59.\n[2] Persi Diaconis and Ron Graham. From Magic to\nMathematics—and Back. To appear.\n[3] Persi Diaconis and Ron Graham. Unpublished manuscript.\n[4] Hal Fredricksen. “A Survey of Full-Length Nonlinear\nShift Register Cycle Algorithms.” SIAM Review 24 (1982),\n195–212.\n[5] Martin Gardner. Fractal Music, Hypercards, and More. .\n. . New York: W. H. Freeman and Company, 1992.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nreference request - Universal cycles for $k$-sets of an $n$-set - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/275049/universal-cycles-for-k-sets-of-an-n-set)\nciteturn10search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; The following conjecture was proposed in [Chung F., Diaconis P., Graham R. ... For every natural $k$ there is $n_0(k)$ such that a universal cycle for $S(n,k)$ always exists provided that $k$ divides $\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$ and $n \\geq n_0(k)$. ... On universal cycles for new classes of combinatorial structures.\n\nEmail\n    9. # Universal cycles for $k$-sets of an $n$-set\n\nAsked 9 years, 3 months ago\n\nModified 9 years, 3 months ago\n\nViewed 179 times\n\nThis question shows research effort; it is useful and clear\n\nLet $[n] = \\{1, \\ldots, n \\}$, and let $S(n,k)$ be the family of $k$-element subsets of $[n]$.\n\nA cyclic word $w$ over alphabet $[n]$ is called a universal cycle for $S(n,k)$ if each element of $S(n,k)$ appears in $w$ as a factor (continuous subword) exatly once.\n\nExamlpe\n\n$n=8$, $k=3$\n\n$w = 02456145712361246703671345034601250135672560234723570147$.\n\nHere, for example, since the first 3-letter subword $024$ represents the 3-set $\\{ 0,2,4 \\}$ then none of the five other 3-letter subwords $042$, $204$, $240$, $402$ and $420$ can occur in $w$.\n\nIt is not hard to see that if $S(n,k)$ has a universal cycle, then $k$ divides $\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$.\n\nThe following conjecture was proposed in [Chung F., Diaconis P., Graham R. Universal cycles for combinatorial structures // Discrete Mathematics (1992)]\n\nConjecture. For every natural $k$ there is $n_0(k)$ such that a universal cycle for $S(n,k)$ always exists provided that $k$ divides $\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$ and $n \\geq n_0(k)$.\n\nI'm wondering if this conjecture is still open, and if yes, then what would be a good reference to check recent progress?\n\nI believe the conjecture is still open. See the comment on Problem 476 and references therein for partial results in the paper below.\n\n> Jackson, Brad; Stevens, Brett; Hurlbert, Glenn. Research problems on Gray codes and universal cycles. Discrete Math. 309 (2009), no. 17, 5341--5348.\n\nThis is fairly recent (2009). A look at the papers which cite the above shows the following paper with some further results.\n\n> Blanca, Antonio; Godbole, Anant P. On universal cycles for new classes of combinatorial structures. SIAM J. Discrete Math. 25 (2011), no. 4, 1832--1842.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFan Chung Graham (https://fanchung.ucsd.edu/cgi-bin/papers.pl?kwd=niver)\nciteturn10search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; (with Jon Folkman and Ron Graham) ... Integers, Combinatorial Number Theory, B. ...   * Universal cycles for combinatorial structures, ... Diaconis and R.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2002 Colloquia | University of Montana (https://www.umt.edu/math/events/colloquia/archive/2002.php)\nciteturn10search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In 1992 Chung, Diaconis and Graham wrote the readable and thoroughly enjoyable \"Universal Cycles for Combinatorial Structures\". ... In their empirical work it was noted and conjectured that a universal cycle for the n – 2-sets of an n-set never exists, even thought the standard necessary conditions are satisfied for all odd n.\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------